WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
When you scroll through a massive feed on a mobile device

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
and the interface violently stutters or completely freezes,

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
you are watching the JavaScript thread buckle

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.000
under the weight of memory allocation.

5
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Rendering hundreds or thousands of elements simultaneously

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.000
is impossible for a phone's limited hardware.

7
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.000
To prevent crashes, developers need absolute,

8
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
deterministic control over the UI thread and memory management.

9
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.000
The solution is virtualization.

10
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
The device only keeps the specific items that are currently on screen

11
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
or just about to enter the viewport, active and memory.

12
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Everything else is discarded.

13
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.000
In Native Android development, this is handled by RecyclerView.

14
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
The central challenge for React Native engineers

15
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000
is replicating that buttery smooth native performance using flat list.

16
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.000
The underlying virtualization principle is exactly the same,

17
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
but the optimization levers are entirely different.

18
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Instead of defining an adapter's item count,

19
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.000
flat list simply reads the erase data length.

20
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Instead of binding a view holder to a position,

21
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.000
you pass a render item function to draw the UI.

22
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Instead of setting a specific item view cache size,

23
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.000
you adjust the window size to buffer off-screen elements,

24
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.000
and instead of relying on a recycled view pool

25
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.000
to manage spatial dimensions,

26
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.000
you use get item layout to bypass rendering calculations.

27
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.000
To build a high performance list engine from first principles,

28
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.000
we have to isolate and tune each of these specific flat list properties.

29
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Translating web-based reactive paradigms

30
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.000
into these strict native memory constraints

31
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
is the only way to achieve fluid scrolling on mobile hardware.

32
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
Let's start when a new data payload arrives,

33
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
triggering a render cycle in the React reconciler.

34
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Without stable identifiers,

35
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
the reconciler cannot track changed elements.

36
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Its default response is to destroy the entire existing branch of nodes

37
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.000
and redraw them from scratch.

38
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.000
In the Android world, this is the equivalent of calling cally data set changed,

39
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.000
a highly inefficient brute force operation

40
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.000
that forces a complete redraw of the list.

41
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
To stop this destructive cycle, you provide the key extractor prop.

42
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.000
By extracting a unique consistent string for every item,

43
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.000
the reconciler pinpoints exact nodes.

44
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Instead of trashing the node,

45
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.000
it updates the specific interior values in place.

46
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.000
This brings the framework up to par with Android's highly efficient notify item changed.

47
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Without those stable keys,

48
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.000
garbage collection overhead will cripple the virtualization engine

49
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.000
before the user even attempts to scroll.

50
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Once the keys are stable,

51
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.000
the next physical limitation occurs

52
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.000
when a user initiates a rapid scroll downward

53
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.000
into an unrendered section of the list.

54
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
This animation shows the default behavior.

55
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Flat list is forced to physically render

56
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.000
and measure the exact pixel height of every single new item

57
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.000
as it crosses into the viewport.

58
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.000
For lists where every row has a fixed height,

59
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.000
this dynamic runtime measurement

60
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.000
is a massive unnecessary drain on processing power.

61
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.000
The override for this is the get item layout prop.

62
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.000
By defining the exact height and offset ahead of time,

63
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.000
you shift the system from reactive DOM measuring

64
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.000
to a strict 01 mathematical calculation.

65
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Knowing exactly where pixel 10,000 will be,

66
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.000
the engine instantly calculates offsets

67
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.000
and jumps to specific indices

68
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.000
without rendering a single proceeding item.

69
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.000
It also enables significantly faster restoration

70
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.000
of the user's initial scroll position

71
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.000
when navigating back from another screen.

72
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Removing layout ambiguity from the UI thread

73
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.000
translates directly to sustained 60 frame per second scrolling.

74
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Even with mathematical layout,

75
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.000
we must look beyond the physical screen.

76
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.000
The window size prop maintains 21 total screens of content,

77
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.000
10 full screens above the viewport,

78
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.000
the active viewport, and 10 screens below.

79
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Lowering this value reduces memory

80
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.000
because it's blank flashes when scrolling fast.

81
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Conversely, increasing window size prevents those flashes,

82
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.000
but massively bloats the application's memory footprint.

83
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.000
The severity of that memory footprint

84
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.000
ultimately depends on the individual row components

85
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.000
returned by the render item function.

86
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Passing an inline arrow function directly to render item

87
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.000
forces react to create a brand new function reference

88
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.000
every single time the parent list renders.

89
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.000
This instantly breaks any memoization

90
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.000
you've applied to those child components.

91
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Rapping the render function in use callback

92
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.000
preserves that reference across renders.

93
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.000
It acts as the react native equivalent

94
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.000
to the native view holder pattern,

95
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.000
protecting the rows from redundant updates.

96
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Balancing a constrained window size

97
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.000
with strict component memoization

98
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.000
will push the standard flat list engine

99
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.000
to its absolute physical limits.

100
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.000
You can perfectly tune a flat list's keys, layouts,

101
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.000
and memory buffers, but a continuous mechanical breakdown

102
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.000
is still occurring just off screen.

103
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.000
Look closely at the left flow chart.

104
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.000
No matter how well optimized,

105
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.000
react handles virtualization

106
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.000
by actively unmounting nodes

107
00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.000
as they exit the top of the buffer

108
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.000
and remounting entirely new nodes

109
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.000
as they enter from the bottom.

110
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.000
They are destroyed and built from scratch,

111
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.000
over and over.

112
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.000
To escape this destructive life cycle,

113
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.000
Shopify engineered a drop-in replacement

114
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.000
called flashlist.

115
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.000
Flashlist abandons the unmount fallacy

116
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.000
in favor of true cell recycling.

117
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.000
As you can see on the right,

118
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.000
when a node leaves the top of the viewport,

119
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.000
it survives.

120
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.000
The empty component shell is physically routed back to the bottom.

121
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.000
That same component shell is kept entirely in memory,

122
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.000
looping infinitely as the user scrolls.

123
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.000
Because the shell is never unmounted,

124
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.000
the UI thread only has to swap out

125
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.000
the interior text and image data.

126
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.000
This achieves exact parity

127
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.000
with how Android's native recycler view operates.

128
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.000
The standard for production applications is straightforward.

129
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.000
If your list exceeds 100 items

130
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.000
or contains complex interactive rows,

131
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.000
you migrate directly to flashlist.

132
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Spending countless development cycles

133
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.000
micro-optimizing the standard flatlist implementation

134
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.000
beyond basic virtualization

135
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.000
is a dead end for enterprise-scale data.

136
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.000
With the list architecture solved,

137
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.000
the logics for animations and gestures

138
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.000
must move off the JavaScript thread entirely,

139
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.000
using reanimated and JSI workloads.

140
00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.000
Achieving a native feel in react native

141
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.000
requires overriding the framework's default DOM life cycles

142
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.000
to respect the physical limits of device memory.

